    "heading": "High Altitude",
    "lineStart": 14070,
    "lineEnd": 14074
  },
  "section": "9.33",
  "rulesText": "Traveling at altitudes of 10,000 feet or higher above sea level is taxing for most creatures because of the reduced amount of oxygen in the air. Each hour such a creature spends traveling at high altitude counts as 2 hours for the purpose of determining how long that creature can travel (see “Travel Pace”).\n\nCreatures can become acclimated to a high altitude by spending 30 days or more at this elevation. Creatures can't become acclimated to elevations above 20,000 feet unless they are native to such environments."
}
